2025-05-01 03:20:51 - Thread-1347 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables and columns, plus why they matter  

• customer_transactions  
  – customer_id:           customer identifier for building individual balances  
  – txn_date:              transaction date used to derive months and the “1‑st of each month” cut‑off  
  – txn_type:              shows whether the amount adds to (e.g. deposit) or removes from (e.g. withdrawal) a balance – lets us assign a sign if needed  
  – txn_amount:            value that will be summed into each customer’s running balance  

Potential filter / join conditions  
• WHERE txn_type IN ('deposit','withdrawal')      – if both appear and we need to treat withdrawals as negative  
• WHERE txn_date <='YYYY‑MM‑01'                   – to obtain balance “as of the 1‑st” of a month  
• GROUP BY strftime('%Y-%m',txn_date)             – to aggregate by month (only illustrative; many engines allow strftime)  
The very first calendar month observed in customer_transactions will be kept only as a baseline (needed to calculate next month’s “previous‑month total”) and then removed from the final listing.

Exploratory SQL queries (LIMIT 20 rows each)  

```sql
-- 1. List a few raw rows to see the general structure of customer_transactions
SELECT *
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Check what transaction types exist and how many of each
SELECT DISTINCT "txn_type"
FROM "customer_transactions";
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect distinct year‑month combinations present in the data
SELECT DISTINCT substr("txn_date",1,7) AS "year_month"
FROM "customer_transactions"
ORDER BY "year_month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Look at the first 20 deposits chronologically
SELECT *
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "txn_type" = 'deposit'
ORDER BY "txn_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Look at the first 20 possible withdrawals (if they exist)
SELECT *
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "txn_type" LIKE '%withdr%'
ORDER BY "txn_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Aggregate total monthly deposited amounts (ignoring withdrawals for now)
SELECT substr("txn_date",1,7) AS "year_month",
       SUM("txn_amount")      AS "total_deposits"
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "txn_type" = 'deposit'
GROUP BY "year_month"
ORDER BY "year_month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Monthly sums split by transaction type to see if withdrawals carry negative values already
SELECT substr("txn_date",1,7) AS "year_month",
       "txn_type",
       SUM("txn_amount") AS "sum_amount"
FROM "customer_transactions"
GROUP BY "year_month","txn_type"
ORDER BY "year_month","txn_type"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Running balance of one sample customer to understand how balances grow
SELECT "customer_id",
       "txn_date",
       "txn_type",
       "txn_amount",
       SUM(CASE WHEN "txn_type"='deposit'  THEN "txn_amount"
                WHEN "txn_type"='withdrawal' THEN -1*"txn_amount"
                ELSE 0 END)
       OVER (PARTITION BY "customer_id"
             ORDER BY "txn_date"
             ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS "running_balance"
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "customer_id" = (SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id"
                       FROM "customer_transactions"
                       LIMIT 1)
ORDER BY "txn_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Total balance for all users as of each individual day
SELECT "txn_date",
       SUM(CASE WHEN "txn_type"='deposit'  THEN  "txn_amount"
                WHEN "txn_type"='withdrawal' THEN -1*"txn_amount"
                ELSE 0 END) AS "net_amount"
FROM "customer_transactions"
GROUP BY "txn_date"
ORDER BY "txn_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Snapshot of balances on the first day of every month
WITH daily_net AS (
      SELECT "txn_date",
             SUM(CASE WHEN "txn_type"='deposit'  THEN  "txn_amount"
                      WHEN "txn_type"='withdrawal' THEN -1*"txn_amount"
                      ELSE 0 END) AS "net_amount"
      FROM "customer_transactions"
      GROUP BY "txn_date"
),
running_total AS (
      SELECT "txn_date",
             SUM("net_amount")
             OVER (ORDER BY "txn_date"
                   ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS "total_balance"
      FROM daily_net
)
SELECT substr("txn_date",1,7) AS "year_month",
       "total_balance"
FROM running_total
WHERE substr("txn_date",9,2) = '01'
ORDER BY "year_month"
LIMIT 20;
```

(The queries above proceed step‑by‑step from basic inspection to a near‑final calculation that will subsequently be adapted to comply with “previous month”, zero‑flooring of negative balances, and exclusion of the very first observed month.)
